A collection loop visits the values in a collection, from the first item to the
last by default.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    names = {"Priya", "Rob", "Emmy"}

    loop names |name|:
        io.line([: Hello, [name]])
    ;
]

The name inside `|...|` is created for the loop body. On each iteration it
refers to the current item.

### Add an index when you need one

A second name receives the item's index in the source.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    names = {"Priya", "Rob", "Emmy"}

    loop names |name, index|:
        io.line([: [index]: [name]])
    ;
]

Without a step, the first item has index `0`, the second has index `1` and so
on. The index binding has type `I32`.

### Visit every second item

Add `by` after the source to set a signed step.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    names = {"Priya", "Rob", "Emmy"}

    loop names by 2 |name, index|:
        io.line([: [index]: [name]])
    ;
]

This visits `Priya` at index `0` and `Emmy` at index `2`. The index binding
still reports the item's real position in the source.

A negative step starts at the last item and walks backwards. The step must be a
signed whole number and cannot be zero.

### Ignore the item when only the repetition matters

Bindings are optional.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    items = {10, 20, 30}
    count ~= 0

    loop items:
        ~count += 1
    ;
]

After this loop, `count` is `3`.
